    <exercise label="Final_20">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Represent the following circuit as a single matrix:
        </p>
        <image source="Final_21.png" />
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1\\0\amp -1\amp 0\amp 1\\-1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\\1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1\\0\amp -1\amp 0\amp 1\\-1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\\1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 0\amp -1\amp 1\\1\amp -1\amp 0\amp 0\\0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 1\\1\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 0\amp 1\amp 1\\-1\amp -1\amp 0\amp 0\\-1\amp 1\amp 0\amp 0\\0\amp 0\amp 1\amp -1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\amp 0\amp 1\\-1\amp 0\amp 1\amp 0\\-1\amp 0\amp -1\amp 0\\0\amp 1\amp 0\amp -1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_21">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For this C-Z (Controlled-Z) gate, which of the following are possible correct start and
          end states? Choose all that apply. Remember from class that phase behavior is best
          understood through matrices. These are not meant to be viewed as independent black and
          white balls, but as different possibilities of a superposition system.
        </p>
        <image source="Final_22.png" />
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>A</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>B</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>C</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>D</p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_22">
      <statement>
        <p>You are setting up a phase kickback circuit, starting with the state <m>\ket{11}</m>. Drag the
          vector notation expression from the left to the expression at the checkpoint on the right.</p>
        <p>Note: Not all expressions on the left are correct!</p>
      </statement>
      <matches>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\0\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
          <response>
            <p>Starting state</p>
          </response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
          <response>
            <p>After initial superposition</p>
          </response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
          <response>
            <p>After phase kickback</p>
          </response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
          <response>
            <p>Ending state</p>
          </response>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\\0\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\begin{bmatrix}0\\0\\1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\1\\1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
        </match>
        <match>
          <premise>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix}1\\1\\-1\\-1\end{bmatrix}</m>
            </p>
          </premise>
        </match>
      </matches>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_23">
      <statement>
        <p>Indicate if the following state is entangled. If it is not, write the individual qubit
          states in 1-qubit bra-ket notation.</p>
        <xi:include href="math_latex_note.ptx" />
        <md>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (\ket{10} - \ket{11})</md>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_24">
      <statement>
        <p>Indicate if the following state is entangled. If it is not, write the individual qubit
          states in 1-qubit bra-ket notation.</p>
        <xi:include href="math_latex_note.ptx" />
        <md>\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} (\ket{10} + \ket{01})</md>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_25">
      <statement>
        <p>Indicate if the following state is entangled. If it is not, write the individual qubit
          states in 1-qubit bra-ket notation.</p>
        <xi:include href="math_latex_note.ptx" />
        <md>\frac{1}{2} (\ket{001} - \ket{011} + \ket{101} - \ket{111})</md>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_26">
      <statement>
        <p>Indicate if the following state is entangled. If it is not, write the individual qubit
          states in 1-qubit bra-ket notation.</p>
        <xi:include href="math_latex_note.ptx" />
        <md>\frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \ket{00} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \ket{01} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{6}} \ket{10} + \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \ket{11}</md>
      </statement>
      <response />
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_27">
      <statement>
        <p>Using the standard quantum teleportation circuit, Alice teleports a qubit <m>\ket{\psi}</m> to Bob.
          During the protocol, Bob receives two bits from Alice: <m>b_1b_0 = 00</m>. When he logged these
          values into his computer, however, he made a mistake and accidentally flipped the bits to <m>b_1b_0 = 11</m>.
          With this incorrect information, Bob performs his normal correction procedures in an
          attempt to recover <m>\ket{\psi}</m>.</p>
        <p>The teleported qubit was intended to have the value of <m>\ket{\psi} = a\ket{0} + b\ket{1}</m>. What is the value of <m>\ket{\psi'}</m>,
          the qubit Bob now has after the possibly incorrect correction procedures were implemented?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{\psi'} = a\ket{0} + b\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{\psi'} = b\ket{0} + a\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{\psi'} = a\ket{0} - b\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <m>\ket{\psi'} = b\ket{0} - a\ket{1}</m>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
    <exercise label="Final_28">
      <statement correct="no">
        <p>Once they have been distributed, you can reuse the same entangled pair of qubits for
          multiple teleportation procedures without bringing them back together again.</p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
